Original commit body from fc5d97a9a2d8ce9a1a74c157e7c9a0adfcb85db3:

TF-IDF cosine on each item's masked full text, top-k neighbours, and
Jarvis-Patrick clustering (an edge only where two items are in each
other's top-k and share at least `--shared` of those neighbours), ranked
by mean pairwise cosine. Pure standard library: the vectorizer reproduces
scikit-learn's TfidfVectorizer(sublinear_tf=True, min_df=2, max_df=0.8)
and the neighbour search is an inverted index. The vocabulary and IDF are
fitted on every non-deleted item; only the selected items (non-resolved
statuses by default; --status, --all) are clustered. 332 open items
against a 2059-item fit: 2.4 s.

Defaults k=6, shared=3 come from a parameter sweep on the tracker (308
clusters, largest 17, 12.5% of within-cluster pairs being declared links
against 0.04% chance; k>=10 chains into one giant component). The method
is the outcome of the 2026-09-04..06 lab-notebook investigation: TF-IDF
cosine beat a dense embedding model, BM25 and BMX at recovering declared
families, and plain top-k neighbours matched or beat a NeighborNet port,
its 96-seed block ensemble with a group-level consensus, and average /
complete linkage at equal pair budgets in about a second. The module
docstring of clusters.py records the chain of evidence.

Tests: sklearn semantics of the vectorizer checked by hand, neighbour
ordering and tie-breaks, Jarvis-Patrick on hand-built neighbour lists,
end-to-end on a two-family corpus, and the subcommand's scope, filters,
JSON and text output; 100% coverage; mypy strict ratchet unchanged.

